  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nSurface low pressure is currently located across southwestern CT\nas of 18Z this afternoon with the associated rain shield\nextending northward through southern and central New England.\nRainfall so far has been confined to south-central NH with\namounts up to around an inch across southwestern NE including\nthe Monadnocks. Latest CAM guidance suggests that this rain\nshield will continue its northward trajectory through the\nremainder of the afternoon and evening but it should dissipate \nsome as it moves north of the mountains given that most forcing \nfor ascent will remain further to the south. \n\nCutoff low pressure will continue to bring periods of moderate\nto locally heavy rainfall through tonight as the SE LLJ\nincreases, allowing for training of convective showers within a\nfavorable environment for heavy precipitation. The area of\ngreatest concern for flash flooding continues to be across \nsouthern NH, particularly in urban areas where there is less \npermeable surfaces as well as across the hilly terrain of \nsouthwestern NH including the Monadnocks. Flash flooding is also\npossible across the Whites where the SE flow will assist in \nupslope precipitation enhancement. Low temperatures tonight will\nbe into the 50s. \n\nConvective showers will persist through the day on Thursday with\nsome breaks mixed in between. Given that soils will be saturated\nin much of southern NH and portions of southwestern ME, will\nneed to monitor for any additional heavy rainfall as that may\nresult in more hydro concerns. Forecast rainfall amounts remain\nlargely unchanged with generally between 1-3\" expected across \nsouth- central NH with the latest 12Z HREF showing the potential\nfor localized amounts of up to around 5\". Rainfall amounts are \nthen expected to drop off quite rapidly north of the mountains \nas well as across much of western ME with some northern \nlocations likely to receive little to no rainfall. High\ntemperatures on Thursday will be limited to the upper 60s/lower\n70s with lows into the 50s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe upper low will be slow to depart on Friday as it moves across \nnorthern New England through the day, gradually meandering east. \nThis pattern will lead to some isolated/scattered showers, \nespecially over New Hampshire, the western Maine mountains, and \ncoastal areas. 12z NAM Nest soundings also suggest that we may have \n200 to 500 J/kg of MUCAPE floating around so some rumbles or thunder \nare not out of the question. Shear will be weak, so this activity \nshould be fairly tame. The upper low finally kicks out in the \nevening/overnight hours. After a cool Thursday, Friday will start a \nwarming trend into the weekend with highs expected to mainly range \nfrom the lower 70s north, to the lower 80s south. \n\nAn upper level ridge axis will then cross the region on Saturday \nwhich will keep the area mostly dry, but a few isolated mountain \nshowers/storms will be possible. Temperatures will increase again on \nSaturday with the ridge moving overhead, with highs expected to \nrange from the mid/upper 70s north, to the low/mid 80s elsewhere.  \n\nOn Sunday, we transition back into southwest flow aloft as the next \ntrough approaches from the west. This will keep temperatures similar \nto Saturday but it could be just a touch cooler as clouds stream \nback in. The next chance for widespread rainfall will then move in \nSunday evening, potentially sticking around through Monday \nevening/night. As of now, this system doesn't look to be a prolific \nrainmaker with NBM probabilities of a half inch or greater only \nmaxing out in the 20 to 40 percent range, but most will see at least \nsome rain with the best chances in the foothill and mountains. After \nthis system exits, the rest of the week looks fairly quiet with \nnormal to above normal temperatures.",
